{
  "level": "c1",
  "target": "de",
  "grammarSchedule": [
    {
      "id": "de.c1.formal-written-connectors",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "mithin, zumal and gleichwohl exist in no other level of the bank and were added for this topic; lesson 1 hands over all three at once. Putting it first is deliberate: every later C1 sentence is written in the register these connectors mark"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.pronominal-adverbs-cohesion",
      "fromLesson": 4,
      "why": "dabei is A2, dadurch and folglich are B2, and demzufolge arrives in lesson 1. The topic is about pointing back at a whole preceding clause, so it needs two clauses worth writing, which the hedging adverbs of lesson 4 supply"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.marked-word-order-emphasis",
      "fromLesson": 6,
      "why": "fronting an object costs no new word at all, only a sentence long enough for the choice to be visible. Lesson 6 opens the academic-verb run, which is where objects worth fronting start to exist"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.multiple-embedded-clauses",
      "fromLesson": 9,
      "why": "nesting a subordinate clause inside another needs three verbs and something to report. The academic verbs of lessons 6 to 9 - behaupten at B2, darlegen, folgern, einräumen here - are exactly the frame verbs that carry the outer clause"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.konjunktiv-i-full-tense-system",
      "fromLesson": 12,
      "why": "B2 taught Konjunktiv I in the present. C1 extends it into the past and the future, which needs no vocabulary but does need reported speech to be automatic first. Twelve lessons is that distance"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.konjunktiv-ii-past-complex",
      "fromLesson": 16,
      "why": "wäre and hätte plus a participle are B1 forms; what is new is nesting the counterfactual inside a longer sentence. Lesson 16 is inside the abstract-noun run, where a condition has something to be about"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.passive-register-choice",
      "fromLesson": 20,
      "why": "all three constructions were taught separately - Vorgangspassiv at B1, Zustandspassiv at B1, sich lassen and -bar at B2. The topic is the choice between them, so it waits until a learner has a register to choose for, which the judging adjectives of lesson 20 establish"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.gerundive-zu-partizip-i",
      "fromLesson": 24,
      "why": "das zu lösende Problem needs Partizip I from B2 and a noun the reader accepts as a task. Lesson 24 sits at the end of the judging-adjectives run, where the attributive slot is already familiar"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.extended-support-verb-constructions",
      "fromLesson": 28,
      "why": "Kritik üben rests on B1 die Kritik and Rücksicht nehmen on B1 die Rücksicht, so the nouns are in hand. B2 taught the pattern with four pairs; C1 is about breadth, and lesson 28 is where the vocabulary to build new pairs from starts arriving"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.combined-modal-particles",
      "fromLesson": 33,
      "why": "the individual particles are A1 to B2 and B2 taught them one at a time. Combining them - ja doch, schon noch - only reads as natural once the surrounding sentence does, which is why this waits past the first thirty lessons"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.sustained-nominal-style",
      "fromLesson": 38,
      "why": "B2 taught nominalization one noun at a time. Sustaining it across a whole sentence needs a chain of genitives, and lesson 38 is deep enough into the distinction-naming run that three abstract nouns can be chained without reaching forward"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.c1.konjunktiv-i-fixed-formulas",
      "fromLesson": 44,
      "why": "man nehme and es lebe are fossils, not a productive rule, so they cost nothing and teach nothing that the other topics need. Placing them late keeps them from being mistaken for the living Konjunktiv I of lesson 12"
    }
  ],
